WEBVTT

00:00:08.895 --> 00:00:09.759
Hi there.

00:00:09.759 --> 00:00:13.759
Welcome to this video where we are going
to learn about misinformation.

00:00:14.607 --> 00:00:19.119
The goal of the video is to help you
to understand what misinformation is,

00:00:19.199 --> 00:00:22.719
what motivates people to do it,
and how to recognize it.

00:00:23.327 --> 00:00:27.199
In the video, firstly,
we will look at what misinformation is.

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:34.079
Then we will look at some examples
of misinformation and why it spreads so easily.

00:00:37.023 --> 00:00:38.655
What is misinformation?

00:00:38.655 --> 00:00:42.223
Misinformation is false
or inaccurate information that is spread

00:00:42.255 --> 00:00:45.263
regardless of whether 
there's an intent to deceive.

00:00:45.583 --> 00:00:49.215
It's essentially information
that isn't true, but the person sharing it

00:00:49.263 --> 00:00:52.655
may genuinely believe it
or may not realize it's false.

00:00:53.535 --> 00:00:56.575
Misinformation spreading 
might happen accidentally,

00:00:56.623 --> 00:00:59.615
without any deliberate intent 
to mislead other people.

00:00:59.903 --> 00:01:03.103
This distinguishes misinformation
from disinformation,

00:01:03.183 --> 00:01:06.463
which is false information
that's deliberately created and spread

00:01:06.463 --> 00:01:09.535
to mislead people with disinformation.

00:01:09.583 --> 00:01:11.903
There's intentional deception involved.

00:01:11.903 --> 00:01:17.055
People spread disinformation doing harm 
to society or some groups of people intentionally.

00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:25.070
Some common examples 
of misinformation include:

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:29.614
sharing outdated facts that were once true
but are no longer accurate,

00:01:30.142 --> 00:01:33.134
misremembered details
that get passed along,

00:01:33.502 --> 00:01:36.494
misleading statistics
taken out of context,

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:40.142
fake news stories shared 
without checking if they are true,

00:01:40.222 --> 00:01:49.182
unverified images or videos shared
as authentic but which have been changed (doctored).

00:01:51.854 --> 00:01:55.774
Misinformation can spread rapidly,
especially on social media,

00:01:55.886 --> 00:01:59.294
because people often share content
without checking its accuracy.

00:02:00.014 --> 00:02:04.000
It can be about any topic health,
politics, science, current events

00:02:04.00 --> 00:02:06.894
and can have real world consequences.

00:02:06.926 --> 00:02:11.326
When people make decisions
based on false information,

00:02:11.406 --> 00:02:14.766
for example, sharing last years 
information about some concert of the star,

00:02:14.926 --> 00:02:18.350
assuming that the news 
is about this year,

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:21.246
or examination requirements
that are not from the examination board.

00:02:21.486 --> 00:02:25.934
The challenge with misinformation is
that it's not always easy to identify,

00:02:26.174 --> 00:02:29.726
and correcting it can be 
difficult once it's widely circulated.

00:02:30.414 --> 00:02:35.214
People tend to remember the initial false
claim even after seeing a correction.

00:02:35.694 --> 00:02:37.054
It is like gossip.

00:02:37.054 --> 00:02:39.806
It spreads fast
and stays for a long period,

00:02:39.806 --> 00:02:42.413
and it is challenging
to make people forget it.

00:02:43.645 --> 00:02:47.325
So in this short video, we learned
what misinformation is and looked

00:02:47.325 --> 00:02:50.685
at some examples of misinformation
and why it spreads so easily.

00:02:51.469 --> 00:02:53.053
Thank you for watching.

00:02:53.053 --> 00:02:55.997
Do not forget to watch
other videos in our series.